    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d347">

                <pb facs="0564" n="518" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg518"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.00/106½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">7 February, 1917</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I am much obliged for
                    this memorandum and entirely agree with Mr. Stabler’s conclusions.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d347-f1"/> The sooner the intimation he suggests giving to the
                    American [<hi rendition="#italic">United?</hi>] Fruit Company is given the
                    better. Their implication lies on the very surface of all the circumstances. A
                    word to Mr. Untermyer, who seems to speak as their attorney, might give them
                    immediate pause.</p>
                <p>I think the telegram to San José ought to be made a little stronger.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d347-f2"/> It ought to instruct the Minister to say to Tinoco that
                    no government set up by him will be recognized, and no government which he takes
                    part in originating or organizing, and that no contracts made by any citizen of
                    the United States with such a government will be recognized by this Government
                    as valid. We cannot be too explicit or too downright. I hope the message will go
                    at the earliest possible moment.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d347-f1" n="1" xml:id="d347-f1-content">
                <p>Memorandum by J. H. Stabler, Division of Latin American Affairs, Department of
                    State; not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d347-f2" n="2" xml:id="d347-f2-content">
                <p>For the telegram as sent, see telegram of Feb. 9, 1917, to the Minister in Costa
                    Rica, <ref target="frus1917#pg_307">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1917, p. 307</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
